{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And of the beast that is not tahor (pure).</b> From here it is proven in Pesaḥim: the verse bent the expression — it counted eight extra letters and did not bring out something disgraceful, for it could have said 'and of the unclean beast.' This is what they said. For we can prove the very same thing from the verse above — ומן הבהמה אשר לא טהורה היא — and there too we find a bent expression, counting eight letters. And one should not answer that this verse — אשר לא טהורה היא — is not a bent expression, on the grounds that the verse speaks of a beast fit for sacrifice, and the reason it writes 'which is not tahor' is to exclude a pure wild animal, and it could not have written 'the teme'ah (unclean)' because 'teme'ah' would imply a pig — this is not so, for it was stated in Zevaḥim: before the Tabernacle was established, all beasts, wild animals, and birds were permitted — males, females, whole ones, blemished ones, pure ones but not impure ones — and all of them as an olah offering. And according to the Talmud, the word <b>tahorah</b> needs to be plene (with a vav) to complete the eight letters of the bent expression."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:03:20.682113823Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}